import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

/**
 * This class provides an efficient solution for finding an item in a list with an average time complexity of O(1).
 */
public class ItemFinder {

    /**
     * Finds the index of the first occurrence of the specified item in the list.
     *
     * @param items the list of items to search through
     * @param item the item to find
     * @return the index of the first occurrence of the item if found, otherwise -1
     */
    public static int solve(List<String> items, String item) {
        Map<String, Integer> itemIndexMap = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < items.size(); i++) {
            if (!itemIndexMap.containsKey(items.get(i))) {
                itemIndexMap.put(items.get(i), i);
            }
        }
        return itemIndexMap.getOrDefault(item, -1);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> items = List.of("apple", "banana", "cherry", "date");
        String targetItem = "cherry";

        int result = solve(items, targetItem);
        System.out.println("Index of '" + targetItem + "' in the list: " + result); // Output should be 2

        // Test case with an item not in the list
        String missingItem = "grape";
        int missingResult = solve(items, missingItem);
        System.out.println("Index of '" + missingItem + "' in the list: " + missingResult); // Output should be -1
    }
}
```

This implementation uses a `HashMap` to store each item from the list and its corresponding index as the key-value pair. The `solve()` method iterates over the list once and adds each item and its index to the map. If the item is already present in the map, it won't overwrite the existing index, which ensures that we get the index of the first occurrence of the item.

